Q: Flattening Code Creates Infinite Loop A: <p>just going through your code i can see that within your submit function you call again the submit function, which leads to an endless recursive behaviour.</p>

<p>so there must be a problem in your <strong>users_model</strong> model on <strong>unlock_user()</strong> function.</p>

<pre><code>if ($this-&gt;is_user_locked($user_data-&gt;lock_date))
  {
   /* User is locked from logging in from too many failed attempts */
   $this-&gt;output('This user account is currently locked!', 'Account Locked', 'Error');
  }
  else
  {
   /* User can be unlocked and form be resubmitted */
   $this-&gt;users_model-&gt;unlock_user($user_data-&gt;user_id);
   $this-&gt;submit($post_username, $post_password); // &lt;- here
   return FALSE;
  }
</code></pre>

Q: Show a modal window without blocking execution of calling code (WPF) A: <p>The WPF libraries, internally, already do both of the things that are needed:</p>
<ul>
<li>Show a modal (input blocking) window.</li>
<li>Show a window and return code execution to the calling method.</li>
</ul>
<p>These two actions are independent of each other (showing a modal window does not inherently require blocking code execution), there just isn't any pre-written method that does both those things.</p>
<p>Digging through the <a href="https://source.dot.net/#PresentationFramework/System/Windows/Window.cs" rel="nofollow noreferrer">.NET source code for the <code>Window</code> class</a>, and taking a look at how <code>ShowDialog</code> works, it looks like some p/invoke (native) method is called in a loop to disable input to all the other windows, then a <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.windows.threading.dispatcherframe" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>DispatcherFrame</code></a> is used to block code execution of the calling method until the new window is closed. When the new window is closed, that p/invoke code is called again to re-enable all the other windows, then <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.windows.threading.dispatcherframe.continue" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>DispatcherFrame.Continue</code></a> is set to false, which yields execution back up the call stack to the caller of <code>ShowDialog</code>.</p>
<p>Now, the <em>right</em> way to solve the posed problem would be to write a new method that does all the same p/invoke shenanigans that <code>ShowDialog</code> does; just duplicate that whole method but WITHOUT the <code>DispatcherFrame</code>. But in reality that 'one method' actually calls a bunch more internal methods and utility methods and it all gets a bit involved. And it all seemed like such a waste to painstakingly duplicate since Microsoft had already done their job writing it.</p>
<p>Instead, there is a much easier 'hack': Use the <code>Loaded</code> event to prematurely end the internal <code>DispatcherFrame</code>. This works out because of the order of method calls in the way Microsoft have structured the code.</p>
<p>DISCLAIMER: For this approach, you have to use a bit of 'evil' reflection to access the private fields of <code>Window</code> you're not supposed to touch. This is BAD PRACTICE because Microsoft could theoretically refactor the way this internal code functions, which would break your code without warning. <em>But</em> if you're willing to bet on the fact that Microsoft won't be changing something as basic as <code>ShowDialog</code>, this approach <em>does</em> save an awful lot of work.</p>
<p>This is the final working code I was able to craft:</p>
<pre><code>public static void ShowModal(this Window window)
{
    window.Loaded += ShowModalWindowLoaded;
    window.Closing += ShowModalWindowClosing;
    window.ShowDialog();
}

private static async void ShowModalWindowLoaded(object sender, RoutedEventArgs e)
{
    var window = (Window)sender;
    window.Loaded -= ShowModalWindowLoaded;

    await window.Dispatcher.BeginInvoke(() =&gt;
    {
        var field = typeof(Window).GetField(&quot;_dispatcherFrame&quot;, System.Reflection.BindingFlags.Instance | System.Reflection.BindingFlags.NonPublic)!;
        var frame = (DispatcherFrame)field.GetValue(window)!;
        frame.Continue = false;
    }, DispatcherPriority.Loaded);
}

private static void ShowModalWindowClosing(object? sender, System.ComponentModel.CancelEventArgs e)
{
    var window = (Window)sender!;
    window.Closing -= ShowModalWindowClosing;

    var field = typeof(Window).GetField(&quot;_showingAsDialog&quot;, System.Reflection.BindingFlags.Instance | System.Reflection.BindingFlags.NonPublic)!;
    field.SetValue(window, true);
}
</code></pre>
<p>From the caller perspective, it's as simple as using the provided extension method, which works on any <code>Window</code>:</p>
<pre><code>myModalPopup.ShowModal()
</code></pre>
<p>In the <code>Loaded</code> event handler for the modal window, I do the following:</p>
<ol>
<li>Use <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.windows.threading.dispatcher.begininvoke" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>Dispatcher.BeginInvoke</code></a> to schedule code to run after the window is actually visible.<br />
Per <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/desktop/wpf/events/object-lifetime-events" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Object lifetime events</a>, the <code>Loaded</code> event occurs just before the actual final rendering of the element. Calling <code>BeginInvoke</code> with <code>DispatcherPriority.Loaded</code> causes the scheduled code to be run after rendering occurs, because <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.windows.threading.dispatcherpriority" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>DispatcherPriority.Loaded &lt; DispatcherPriority.Render</code></a>. This ensures that control does not return to the caller of <code>ShowModal</code> before the window is actually visible. (Note: At this point, the internal <code>DispatcherFrame</code> is still blocking code execution, so this un-awaited <code>async void ShowModalWindowLoaded</code> doesn't cause issues.)</li>
<li>Use reflection to get the internal <code>DispatcherFrame</code> object from the private field.</li>
<li>Set <code>DispatcherFrame.Contuine</code> to false to yield code execution back to the caller of <code>ShowModal</code> while the window is still visible.</li>
</ol>
<p>As for the <code>Closing</code> event handler:</p>
<p><code>_showingAsDialog</code> is the private field that the <code>Window</code> uses to keep track of whether it was opened using <code>Show</code> or <code>ShowDialog</code>. This is the field which is checked, upon the close of the window, to determine whether the other windows in the application need to be re-enabled.</p>
<p><code>_showingAsDialog</code> is set to false as part of the <code>ShowDialog</code> method, right at the end after the internal <code>DispatcherFrame</code> ends. Normally, this happens <em>after</em> the window closes. But because we are ending the <code>DispatcherFrame</code> prematurely, we need to reset <code>_showingAsDialog</code> to true before the window closes, so that the closing of the window properly re-enables the other windows.</p>
